Notebook[{
Cell["\<\
Build a Large Language Model from Scratch \[LongDash] in Wolfram Language\
\>", "Title",ExpressionUUID->"21738e24-66ae-4b62-935c-150201b6275e"],

Cell["Part 3: The full transformer block + parameter accounting", "Subtitle",ExpressionUUID->"36413683-bc24-4268-9c57-9e69d3779bd2"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Parts 1 and 2 produced two pieces: a tokeniser/embedder that turns ",
 "text into a (seqLen, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "model"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"03c9ba87-2f73-4116-844b-ee7f774a320f"],
 ") tensor (Part 1), and a multi-head causal self-attention block ",
 "that takes such a tensor and returns a tensor of the same shape ",
 "(Part 2). Part 3 wraps that attention block inside the canonical ",
 "transformer envelope and stacks the result ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["n", "layers"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"f845ce4a-155e-444a-8651-7cb03dd28db4"],
 " times. We pick model sizes for Part 4's training runs, derive a ",
 "closed-form parameter-count formula, and verify it empirically."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"5496b924-45bc-4d71-bb13-8300a45d1404"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Import", "[", "\"\<figures/illustration_block_diagram.png\>\"", 
  "]"}]], "Input",ExpressionUUID->"c811edea-d24c-42a8-8a7f-dafd09dc79f7"],

Cell["1.  Setup", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"dda8bcd5-3786-49e3-a7e2-a8c10d2d5529"],

Cell["\<\
We need the Tokeniser package from Part 1 and the Attention package from Part \
2. The new Transformer package on top of them adds the feed-forward block, \
the full transformer block, and the model constructor.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"6f75a877-560c-4fea-b314-0e3887835ce5"],

Cell["SetDirectory[NotebookDirectory[]]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"075d3f83-d4e0-495a-afbf-38a475dff29b"],

Cell["\<\
Get[\"../../part-01-tokeniser-and-embeddings/src/Tokeniser.wl\"]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"7412ea9c-e001-42f0-86fd-aa161b238ef5"],

Cell["Get[\"../../part-02-self-attention/src/Attention.wl\"]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"016f8586-e596-4bad-9c79-373862cf29ff"],

Cell["Get[\"src/Transformer.wl\"]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"a815c1f7-2b02-4591-ae49-df45ece640e4"],

Cell["2.  Position-wise feed-forward block", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"783a9bc9-6bb3-4872-9912-ab2fa1a7148f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The attention block lets every position look at every other position. ",
 "But within each position, all it does is take a weighted average of ",
 "value vectors. There is no per-position transformation that mixes the ",
 "features of a single token nonlinearly \[LongDash] no place for the ",
 "model to actually ",
 StyleBox["compute",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " on what it has gathered. The feed-forward block fixes that: a ",
 "two-layer position-wise MLP with a non-linearity in the middle."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"9378adb6-0eb6-46ae-9ae7-bca40739eeb4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Two linear projections sandwich a GELU non-linearity: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "model"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"7f19c7e9-1c7e-4722-a6c9-738fe177f18d"],
 " \[Rule] ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "ff"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"b7c94262-72c3-4fcf-90fa-9454cef09731"],
 " \[Rule] ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "model"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"49579c76-fdaf-44eb-9d87-a703309772f4"],
 ". Conventionally ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "ff"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"7c36e95b-674e-415b-9edd-a1f6d184b270"],
 " = 4 \[Times] ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "model"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"14a3bc07-c472-4d18-9d90-fb32f8db7448"],
 "; this is where most of the model's parameter budget lives. The ",
 "block is applied independently to every position of the sequence, ",
 "hence wrapped in ",
 StyleBox["NetMapOperator",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 "."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"51b5064b-eddf-42c7-bc1b-657eb8064e8f"],

Cell["\<\
ffn = LLMAtoZ`Transformer`feedForwardBlock[64, 256, 16];
SeedRandom[1]; x = RandomReal[NormalDistribution[0, 0.1], {16, 64}];
Dimensions[ffn[x]]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"c4697f34-0744-448b-8f56-d8914a85ceda"],

Cell["3.  The transformer block, pre-norm style", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"b3f734dc-9110-426d-9a0a-bffaa682d68a"],

Cell["Two sub-layers, each in its own residual connection:", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"22585606-5b2f-47d2-869e-394c2b241acd"],

Cell["\<\
    x' = x + Attention(LayerNorm(x))
    y  = x' + FFN(LayerNorm(x'))\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"95b2dd0c-5aaa-4c6c-81f0-23fd7bad0070"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Two design choices visible here. First, the LayerNorm sits ",
 StyleBox["before",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " each sub-layer, not after. This is the ",
 StyleBox["pre-norm",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " ordering, which differs from the post-norm ordering in the original ",
 "Vaswani et al. paper. We will look at the gradient-flow argument for ",
 "pre-norm in a moment. Second, each sub-layer is wrapped in an ",
 "additive residual \[LongDash] the sub-layer learns what to ",
 StyleBox["add",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " to the residual stream, not what to replace it with. The original ",
 "ResNet argument applies: when a sub-layer's right answer is \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]do nothing\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote], it can learn ",
 "to output zero rather than to learn the identity transformation."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e0cd46bb-3b09-4ebb-9d35-bc1cef96580c"],

Cell["\<\
blk = LLMAtoZ`Transformer`transformerBlock[64, 4, 256, 16];
blk[x] // Dimensions\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"47a44292-b919-448d-873c-c404e774c936"],

Cell["Information[blk, \"ArraysTotalElementCount\"]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"f3f7db0e-d4c4-42ec-9d1a-96317c7b2f2c"],

Cell["4.  Pre-norm vs post-norm", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"b7021109-9409-4aa1-857b-c487cef5f3ce"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Why pre-norm? In a deep stack, what matters is whether the residual ",
 "stream is ever destroyed. Pre-norm normalises the ",
 StyleBox["input",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " to each sub-layer, then adds the sub-layer's output to the unnormalised ",
 "residual. The residual stream is never normalised, so a gradient flowing ",
 "backward through ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["n", "layers"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"ee16787c-81a9-4b9d-975c-9c3193f3e580"],
 " layers is the product of (1 + small_correction) factors, which is a ",
 "well-behaved O(1) number. Post-norm normalises ",
 StyleBox["after",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " the residual addition, so each layer rescales the residual stream by ",
 "1/std, and a gradient flowing backward is a product of repeatedly-rescaled ",
 "numbers \[LongDash] which can compress to zero quickly."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"37235d8c-2041-42cd-b824-0170ae5e6a63"],

Cell["\<\
A synthetic illustration of the difference on randomly-initialised stacks (no \
training, just forward-pass activation norms):\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d2bf375f-3713-4cd5-9850-1263e1a3ebcb"],

Cell["Import[\"figures/pre_vs_post_norm_gradients.png\"]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"e0a681aa-2405-4e4e-8233-e85030f4a7a2"],

Cell["\<\
Pre-norm grows linearly in depth (because each residual addition adds a small \
random vector to an unnormalised stream); post-norm flattens because the \
LayerNorm at the end of every block keeps the stream's magnitude pinned to 1. \
The reverse situation in backprop is what makes post-norm hard to train at \
depth without warmup tricks. Modern transformers (GPT-2/3, LLaMA, Mistral, \
nanochat) all use pre-norm.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d4b702ea-6075-4eca-a779-ad7805ba4715"],

Cell["5.  The full GPT model", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"f3b07e16-6c67-4224-a698-7fb38ae6331d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Putting it together: token embedder + ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["n", "layers"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"eaf090c2-db42-4597-823b-3430e170e717"],
 " transformer blocks + a final LayerNorm + an output projection of ",
 "shape (",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "model"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"d1cea4c2-e430-4938-97c6-db315acaff5f"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"|", "V", "|"}]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"f1914f54-c1d1-4d82-9443-e5de852906ea"],
 ") that produces a logit at every ",
 "position for every token in the vocabulary."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ad540f13-e55c-4c03-8192-81079619b858"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "One small WL detail: the embedder from Part 1 has two input ports ",
 "(\"tokens\" and \"positions\"), which a ",
 StyleBox["NetChain",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " cannot consume directly. The Transformer package exports a ",
 StyleBox["chainEmbedder",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " that uses ",
 StyleBox["SequenceIndicesLayer",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " to generate the positions internally, so we can drop the embedder ",
 "into a chain along with the blocks."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"03fc1f9c-dcb8-4a39-937a-c54959183148"],

Cell["\<\
model = LLMAtoZ`Transformer`gptModel[256, 8, 6, 1024, 1064, 256];
ids = RandomInteger[{1, 1064}, 256];
Dimensions[model[ids]]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"cc837029-3378-4511-89ae-88a2c45ebbab"],

Cell["\<\
(seqLen, |V|) out, as promised. Every row of the output is the pre-softmax \
logit vector at one position. During training we shift it by one and ask the \
model to predict the next token. During inference we apply a softmax to the \
last row and sample. Both come in Parts 4 and 5.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"89c69c20-6331-4db0-a4b7-11433b86045f"],

Cell["6.  Counting parameters", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"6684cdc1-5f27-426b-b26b-92147dc423ff"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A pre-norm transformer block of size (",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "model"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"a53ec639-c46c-457f-ae16-9eaffbc2546c"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "ff"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"ff8b3141-7b07-4b33-ab4e-5a3c1023189b"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["n", "heads"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"53a532c6-be08-49cf-b7b7-7a72cef562d5"],
 "):"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e602c55c-e58b-4dd5-9af7-7e82f5a01807"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\[FilledSmallCircle] Q, K, V projections summed over heads: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"3", 
   SuperscriptBox[
    SubscriptBox["d", "model"], "2"]}]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"88032b01-6e80-4e2d-a785-e9cf45fd7d9f"],
 " (no biases by convention).\n",
 "\[FilledSmallCircle] Output projection: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SuperscriptBox[
   SubscriptBox["d", "model"], "2"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"5af02eb5-a5f9-4127-8444-c9a3e063406c"],
 ".\n",
 "\[FilledSmallCircle] Two LayerNorms (scale + bias each): ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"4", 
   SubscriptBox["d", "model"]}]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"90c2dc61-3882-45b8-838e-5f2328acb2e6"],
 ".\n",
 "\[FilledSmallCircle] FFN linear 1 (with bias): ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox["d", "model"], 
   SubscriptBox["d", "ff"], 
   RowBox[{" ", 
    RowBox[{"+", " "}]}], 
   SubscriptBox["d", "ff"]}]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"0f5566ce-12c5-4f55-8d6f-b5f7b65afd6a"],
 ".\n",
 "\[FilledSmallCircle] FFN linear 2 (with bias): ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox["d", "ff"], 
   SubscriptBox["d", "model"], 
   RowBox[{" ", 
    RowBox[{"+", " "}]}], 
   SubscriptBox["d", "model"]}]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"1eb5ddc2-d6a1-4157-8ea4-8d921757c6e3"],
 "."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"616ca99a-2c74-4e35-b7a6-2798040723c1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Per block: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"4", 
   SuperscriptBox[
    SubscriptBox["d", "model"], "2"], 
   RowBox[{" ", 
    RowBox[{"+", " "}]}], "2", 
   SubscriptBox["d", "model"], 
   SubscriptBox["d", "ff"], 
   RowBox[{" ", 
    RowBox[{"+", " "}]}], 
   SubscriptBox["d", "ff"], 
   RowBox[{" ", 
    RowBox[{"+", " "}]}], "5", 
   SubscriptBox["d", "model"]}]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"00737e3f-7ba6-404a-878d-56742b1eebc3"],
 ". And on top of that, embeddings (",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox["d", "model"], 
   RowBox[{"(", 
    RowBox[{"|", "V", "|"}], 
    RowBox[{" ", 
     RowBox[{"+", " "}]}], 
    SubscriptBox["n", "max"], ")"}]}]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"c9d71166-afe2-4ac2-8e48-2062185b15f4"],
 "), a final LayerNorm (2",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "model"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"0e3afd04-d605-4eba-b867-fe35c58377e2"],
 "), and an output projection ",
 "(",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox["d", "model"], 
   RowBox[{"|", "V", "|"}], 
   RowBox[{" ", 
    RowBox[{"+", " "}]}], 
   RowBox[{"|", "V", "|"}]}]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"98e225d1-ea15-43d2-91cf-3807e3da949f"],
 ")."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"a6b1a812-ecda-4bd8-8497-db6c171b4c58"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The exported function ",
 StyleBox["parameterCount[spec]",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " computes this closed form; ",
 StyleBox["parameterCountFull[spec]",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " adds the frozen causal-mask arrays we bake into each attention head ",
 "(one ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["n", "heads"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"ace5f987-736c-49b5-b6ce-78643ad74534"],
 " \[Times] (maxSeqLen)\[Times](maxSeqLen) tensor ",
 "per layer), which is what WL's ",
 StyleBox["Information[net, \"ArraysTotalElementCount\"]",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " counts."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0cb3e173-8b13-484a-84e6-2e08feccd5bb"],

Cell["\<\
spec = LLMAtoZ`Transformer`modelSpec5M[1064, 256];
trainable = LLMAtoZ`Transformer`parameterCount[spec];
full = LLMAtoZ`Transformer`parameterCountFull[spec];
actual = Information[
  LLMAtoZ`Transformer`gptModel[spec[\"dModel\"], spec[\"nHeads\"], \
spec[\"nLayers\"],
    spec[\"dFF\"], spec[\"vocabSize\"], spec[\"maxSeqLen\"]],
  \"ArraysTotalElementCount\"];
{trainable, full, actual}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"7c83cd4c-d6b1-424e-9d3f-d6bbbac63e3a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Trainable, full (with frozen masks), and actual agree exactly. The ",
 "frozen masks are non-trainable arrays the optimizer never touches; ",
 "they exist only because we stored the causal mask as a constant ",
 "inside the attention NetGraph. By convention we name our models by ",
 "their ",
 StyleBox["trainable",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " parameter count, so the 5M model truly has 5.3M trainable params."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"cf31394f-f309-44e7-8370-dedf32ad1c4e"],

Cell["Where do those parameters live?", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"3681c4fb-5ea2-4df7-adb6-1a57da012bde"],

Cell["Import[\"figures/params_breakdown.png\"]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"bad01db0-3b57-4045-aa78-a3b883000855"],

Cell["\<\
Most of the trainable parameters (37%) live in the FFN; the frozen causal \
masks contribute another 37% of the array count even though they are not \
trainable. The attention QKV projections account for 14%. The embedding \
tables, output projection, and LayerNorms contribute the remaining low \
single-digit percentages. This is the shape one finds across most transformer \
sizes; the picture changes for very small models where the embedding tables \
dominate, and for very large models where attention QKV catches up to FFN.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"9d9b0106-d12d-4c89-9454-2f5cd592e865"],

Cell["7.  Three sizes for Part 4", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"a18f75ac-a266-4161-abc0-e9296845beea"],

Cell["\<\
The training run in Part 4 needs a small ladder of model sizes so we can \
recover a Chinchilla-style loss-vs-compute signature. The package exports \
three reference specs at the Tiny Shakespeare scale (|V| = 1064, maxSeqLen = \
256):\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"044842a0-3997-4d96-a149-7aded48b3efb"],

Cell["\<\
Grid[
  Prepend[
    Table[
      With[{s = ms},
        {s[\"label\"], s[\"dModel\"], s[\"nHeads\"], s[\"nLayers\"], \
s[\"dFF\"],
         LLMAtoZ`Transformer`parameterCount[s],
         LLMAtoZ`Transformer`parameterCountFull[s]}],
      {ms, LLMAtoZ`Transformer`modelSizeSweep[1064, 256]}],
    {\"label\", \"d_model\", \"n_heads\", \"n_layers\", \"d_ff\",
     \"trainable\", \"with frozen masks\"}],
  Frame -> All, Background -> {None, {LightGray, None, None}},
  Alignment -> Center, Spacings -> {1.5, 0.5}]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"c99f0246-d533-4698-b770-2ed14dfd3a7d"],

Cell["7.1  Interactive: dial your own model", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"6ca5b4a1-7400-4789-9c78-088abafc017d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Pick any (",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "model"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"083beaf5-9a4c-43c6-92ab-5739d8b93ea3"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "ff"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"6218353d-92ce-47ec-b8ea-e358d04ca808"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["n", "layers"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"23547f7c-b846-4c3f-9368-fba6815b9329"],
 ") combination ",
 "and read off the parameter count on the fly. Common ratios: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "ff"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"c1606050-ead8-4c5c-856b-7ec88ae0aec2"],
 " = 4",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "model"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"ab895f25-501b-4a59-b7ee-9055787e9b34"],
 " for standard transformers; ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "ff"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"a9487b7a-8943-4097-8770-9d2d4d9ecd8c"],
 " between 2",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "model"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"59172055-45be-4053-8cb7-e53fffc87afc"],
 " and 4",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "model"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"0ed4fa4c-1f1d-48e9-8a5c-a05f79d464ec"],
 " for nanochat and recent compact architectures."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"a6834959-500f-4451-a65e-887a592558ad"],

Cell["\<\
Manipulate[
  With[{spec = <|\"dModel\" -> d, \"nHeads\" -> h, \"nLayers\" -> L,
                 \"dFF\" -> f, \"vocabSize\" -> v, \"maxSeqLen\" -> n|>},
    Column[{
      Style[Row[{\"trainable params: \",
        Style[NumberForm[LLMAtoZ`Transformer`parameterCount[spec], DigitBlock \
-> 3],
              Bold, 18, ColorData[97][1]]}], 14],
      Style[Row[{\"with frozen masks: \",
        Style[NumberForm[LLMAtoZ`Transformer`parameterCountFull[spec], \
DigitBlock -> 3],
              Bold, 14, ColorData[97][2]]}], 12],
      Style[Row[{\"size ratio FFN/attention: \",
        NumberForm[N[(2 d f + f + d) / (4 d^2 + 4 d)], 3]}], 12, Gray]
    }]
  ],
  {{d, 256, \"d_model\"}, 64, 1024, 64, Appearance -> \"Labeled\"},
  {{h, 8, \"n_heads\"}, 4, 16, 4, Appearance -> \"Labeled\"},
  {{L, 6, \"n_layers\"}, 1, 24, 1, Appearance -> \"Labeled\"},
  {{f, 1024, \"d_ff\"}, 256, 4096, 256, Appearance -> \"Labeled\"},
  {{v, 1064, \"|V|\"}, 256, 50000, 256, Appearance -> \"Labeled\"},
  {{n, 256, \"max_seq_len\"}, 64, 2048, 64, Appearance -> \"Labeled\"},
  ControlPlacement -> Top]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"2928bc93-8f85-4fb2-969a-f361d4241fae"],

Cell["8.  Reconstructing GPT-2 from our primitives", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"2b666966-9d25-4d70-9055-36f158257fe2"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A natural question: is our ",
 StyleBox["gptModel",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " constructor really general, or is it a toy that only handles the ",
 "1M/5M/25M reference sizes? The answer: plug in GPT-2 small's ",
 "architectural spec and you get GPT-2 small \[Dash] the same ",
 "architecture OpenAI shipped in 2019, parameter-count exact."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"93be9914-16d1-4539-ad9f-746402938ed3"],

Cell["\<\
spec = LLMAtoZ`Transformer`gpt2SmallSpec[];
spec\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"0ba16177-8e50-4694-8c85-1e4cd5ec1549"],

Cell["\<\
Without weight tying the parameter formula gives ~163M; with weight tying \
(sharing the input-embedding matrix with the output projection, transposed) \
it gives 124.45M, matching real GPT-2 small (124.44M) to within ~13K \
parameters. The residual is a tiny convention gap in how LayerNorm biases are \
counted.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"94b3a934-aa4e-4c4b-baaa-a2b4e9761848"],

Cell["\<\
{LLMAtoZ`Transformer`parameterCount[spec],
 LLMAtoZ`Transformer`parameterCount[spec, \"WeightTying\" -> True]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"b7949e00-9dc8-4a62-b5ba-957fd2bc5102"],

Cell["\<\
Two equivalent ways forward exist for the rest of the series:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"583755dd-a10b-4568-abd9-06e129003562"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\[FilledCircle] ",
 StyleBox["Path A: build from scratch.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " ",
 StyleBox["buildGpt2Architecture[\"Small\"]",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " constructs an empty 124M-parameter net at GPT-2's spec. You then ",
 "train it from random init (Part 4 onward). On a Mac mini this is ",
 "infeasible \[LongDash] but the architectural code is yours.\n",
 "\[FilledCircle] ",
 StyleBox["Path B: load the pretrained NetModel.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " ",
 StyleBox["loadPrebuiltGpt2[]",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " returns OpenAI's trained checkpoint via WL's Neural Network ",
 "Repository (498 MB on first download). You skip pretraining and ",
 "fine-tune from real pretrained weights (Part 6 onward), which is ",
 "what the cloud-cheap and cloud-real tiers actually do."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"4540761c-d1e5-4410-9664-3f955234ba29"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We will choose between these in ",
 StyleBox["Tiers.wl",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " (introduced in Part 4): a single named tier sets the model spec, ",
 "the training corpus, and whether to use ",
 StyleBox["buildGpt2Architecture",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " or ",
 StyleBox["loadPrebuiltGpt2",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " as the starting point."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e97e6aae-2c4a-4e63-a7e3-c8c76a51034a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A sanity check we ",
 StyleBox["can",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " do on a Mac mini: compare the two architectures' top-level ",
 "structure without instantiating the full 124M net."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"69707f1d-06aa-4a35-8001-89b6ac6daf31"],

Cell["\<\
(* Don't build the full 124M net at notebook-eval time;
   it's slow and memory-hungry.  Inspect the spec instead. *)
specSummary = <|
  \"OurArchitecture\" -> \"gptModel[gpt2SmallSpec]: 12-layer pre-norm,
     12 heads, d_model 768, d_ff 3072, vocab 50257, max_seq 1024.\",
  \"OpenAIsArchitecture\" -> \"GPT-2 small: 12-layer post-norm or
     pre-norm (replications vary), 12 heads, d_model 768, d_ff 3072,
     vocab 50257, max_seq 1024.\",
  \"Differences\" -> {
    \"Our attention uses 12 separate (d_head x d_model) Q/K/V projections;
     OpenAI's checkpoint stores them as a single (d_model x d_model)
     matrix.  Mathematically equivalent; the reshape happens at load.\",
    \"Our output emits per-position logits (seqLen x |V|) for parallel
     training; the NetModel emits softmax probabilities at the last
     position only.  Either form is a one-line wrap of the other.\"
  }|>;
specSummary\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"c30716b3-e144-4987-a43a-60b467811894"],

Cell["9.  Summary and what comes next", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"909dd5e7-2bdc-4825-9ffc-6fd42e41cf6c"],

Cell["\<\
We finish Part 3 with seven deliverables, each reusable in the remaining \
parts:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"4d06020b-abd5-4faa-9db8-52a9e81b4538"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\[FilledSquare] ",
 StyleBox["A pre-norm transformer block.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " ",
 StyleBox["transformerBlock[dModel, nHeads, dFF, maxSeqLen]",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " returns a six-node NetGraph: norm \[Rule] attention \[Rule] add \[Rule] \
norm \[Rule] FFN \[Rule] add. ",
 "Pre-norm is the canonical 2020+ choice for stable deep training.\n",
 "\[FilledSquare] ",
 StyleBox["A position-wise feed-forward block.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " ",
 StyleBox["feedForwardBlock[dModel, dFF, maxSeqLen]",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " is the standard ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "model"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"75b0cc7b-5f0c-41c7-9d0c-8a87de267310"],
 " \[Rule] ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "ff"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"db882012-b97f-4440-ab43-85ea80885657"],
 " \[Rule] (GELU) \[Rule] ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "model"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"106e42ce-29fe-47fc-8f01-cd9e81fafc7a"],
 " sandwich, applied per position.\n",
 "\[FilledSquare] ",
 StyleBox["A full GPT model constructor.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " ",
 StyleBox["gptModel[dModel, nHeads, nLayers, dFF, vocabSize, maxSeqLen]",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " returns a NetChain from token IDs to per-position logits, ready to ",
 "train.\n",
 "\[FilledSquare] ",
 StyleBox["A closed-form parameter count.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " ",
 StyleBox["parameterCount[spec]",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["parameterCountFull[spec]",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " agree exactly with the empirical ",
 StyleBox["Information",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " count for all three of our reference model sizes.\n",
 "\[FilledSquare] ",
 StyleBox["A model-size ladder.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " ",
 StyleBox["modelSpec1M",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["modelSpec5M",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["modelSpec25M",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " return ready-to-use specs for the Part 4 training experiments. ",
 "(Trainable parameter counts: 1.10M, 5.34M, 18.65M.)\n",
 "\[FilledSquare] ",
 StyleBox["An interactive size dial.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " Section 7.1's ",
 StyleBox["Manipulate",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " lets the reader pick any (",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "model"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"487b0dfa-c95d-4a87-b274-aeeb70698b75"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["n", "heads"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"05a28c81-db19-4d1a-b436-14d846e8f042"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["n", "layers"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"be18a641-9599-4fab-9ccd-8aeb77177cc2"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox["d", "ff"]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"f891e2df-b859-4989-ba8f-3860aa2c5595"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"|", "V", "|"}]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"997b4167-3902-495b-8672-49d0364c0d30"],
 ", maxSeqLen) and see the parameter count, the with-mask ",
 "count, and the FFN/attention size ratio update in real time.\n",
 "\[FilledSquare] ",
 StyleBox["GPT-2 reconstruction.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " ",
 StyleBox["gpt2SmallSpec[]",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " plugged into our same ",
 StyleBox["gptModel",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " constructor yields a parameter count exact to within 13K of ",
 "real GPT-2 small (with weight tying). The same constructor at our ",
 "1M spec gives the toy model Part 4 actually trains.\n",
 "\[FilledSquare] ",
 StyleBox["Use-the-prebuilt-or-build-your-own switch.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " ",
 StyleBox["loadPrebuiltGpt2[]",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " returns the trained NetModel checkpoint for fine-tuning ",
 "starts; ",
 StyleBox["buildGpt2Architecture[]",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " returns an empty net at the same spec for train-from-scratch ",
 "tiers."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"32a092b8-659e-40da-ba4b-1ef69be8ecad"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In ",
 StyleBox["Part 4",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " we train the 1M and 5M models on Tiny Shakespeare with a CPU-only ",
 "loop running on a Mac mini, plot the loss curve live via ",
 StyleBox["TrainingProgressFunction",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 ", and offer the cloud path: submit the 25M model on a FineWeb-Edu ",
 "slice via ",
 StyleBox["RemoteBatchSubmit",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " for readers with a Wolfram Cloud account. The headline figure is ",
 "the loss vs FLOPs overlay across all three sizes, recovering a ",
 "small-scale Chinchilla signature."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"a67d51ac-6c20-40ec-ace2-7c41ef0ffb6a"],

Cell["References", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"bf5b29b7-7e10-4fc5-be16-471b5fc32f99"],

Cell["\<\
Vaswani, A. et al. (2017). Attention Is All You Need. NeurIPS.
Xiong, R. et al. (2020). On Layer Normalization in the Transformer \
Architecture. ICML.
Hendrycks, D., Gimpel, K. (2016). Gaussian Error Linear Units (GELUs).
Hoffmann, J. et al. (2022). Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models. \
(Chinchilla.)
Karpathy, A. (2025). nanochat. github.com/karpathy/nanochat.
Raschka, S. (2024). Build a Large Language Model from Scratch. Manning.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"3195cd7c-7430-4978-9c5d-9a6e62d690eb"]
},
Saveable->True,
WindowSize->{900, 750},
FrontEndVersion->"14.3 for Mac OS X ARM (64-bit) (July 8, 2025)",
StyleDefinitions->"Default.nb",
ExpressionUUID->"43dd4f8a-090f-457b-b6af-b31b58257ae4"
]
